+\subsection{Constructing and testing a marginal profile}
+
+Theorem~\ref{density-universal-profiles} has a direct probabilistic
+interpretation.  First choose a matching $M$ with probabilities $w_M$.
+For each $\{i,j\}\in M$, draw $(X_i,X_j)$ with joint density $V_{ij}$
+relative to $\mu_i\otimes\mu_j$, independently for the different
+matched pairs; draw every unmatched coordinate from its original law.
+The two constant marginal integrals of $V_{ij}$ ensure that each
+coordinate retains its original distribution.  A specified pair is
+drawn from its prescribed joint law with probability $\lambda_{ij}$,
+and otherwise has the independent product law.  This gives exactly
+$1+\lambda_{ij}(V_{ij}-1)$ as its density.  Thus a matching
+decomposition is an explicit sampling rule, and the same decomposition
+works for every collection of the pair kernels.  These mean-one
+densities need not be bounded by one; bounded kernels are considered
+separately in the density estimates below.
+
+\proofpart{Three and four coordinates}
+For $r=3$, the matching polytope is simply
+\[
+ \lambda_{12},\lambda_{13},\lambda_{23}\ge0,
+ \qquad \lambda_{12}+\lambda_{13}+\lambda_{23}\le1.
+\]
+Writing $s=\lambda_{12}+\lambda_{13}+\lambda_{23}$, the construction
+is the explicit density
+\[
+ U=1-s+\lambda_{12}V_{12}+\lambda_{13}V_{13}
+           +\lambda_{23}V_{23}.
+\]
+In particular, three copies of the same symmetric kernel with equal
+coefficient $1/3$ are realized by
+$(V(x_1,x_2)+V(x_1,x_3)+V(x_2,x_3))/3$.
+
+For $r=4$, the only constraints besides nonnegativity are the four
+degree inequalities and the four triangle inequalities.  They also
+give a closed formula for the construction.  Partition the six edges
+into the three pairs of opposite edges
+\[
+ \mathcal P=\{\{12,34\},\{13,24\},\{14,23\}\}.
+\]
+For $\{e,f\}\in\mathcal P$, put
+$a_{ef}=\min\{\lambda_e,\lambda_f\}$, and put
+$w_0=1-\sum_{\{e,f\}\in\mathcal P}
+                \max\{\lambda_e,\lambda_f\}$.
+Then
+\begin{equation}\label{flesh-profile-four-formula}
+ U=w_0+\sum_{\{e,f\}\in\mathcal P}
+ \left[a_{ef}V_eV_f+
+       (\lambda_e-a_{ef})V_e+(
+       \lambda_f-a_{ef})V_f\right].
+\end{equation}
+Here $V_{ij}$ is evaluated at $(x_i,x_j)$.
+To see that $w_0\ge0$, choosing one edge from each opposite pair
+gives one of the four three-edge stars or four triangles of $K_4$.
+The sum of the three maxima is therefore the largest of their eight
+coefficient sums, each at most one.  The coefficients in
+\eqref{flesh-profile-four-formula} are nonnegative and sum to one;
+they give weights on the empty matching, the singleton matchings
+and the three perfect matchings.  Each edge has total inclusion
+probability $\lambda_e$, which verifies all six marginals.
+At the common coefficient $1/3$, this reduces to
+\[
+ U=\frac13\left(V_{12}V_{34}+V_{13}V_{24}+V_{14}V_{23}\right).
+\]
+
+The triangle constraints cannot be omitted.  For instance, the
+profile $\lambda_{12}=\lambda_{13}=\lambda_{23}=1/2$, with all
+other coefficients zero, satisfies every degree inequality but
+has no universal realization.  Already the common two-point kernel
+$V(a,b)=1-ab$, for uniform $a,b\in\{-1,1\}$, would require
+\[
+ \mathbb E(Z_1+Z_2+Z_3)^2
+  =3-2(\lambda_{12}+\lambda_{13}+\lambda_{23})=0,
+\]
+although the sum of three signs is never zero.  More generally,
+on five coordinates the common coefficient $1/4$ satisfies all
+degree and triangle inequalities but violates the five-element
+odd-set inequality.  The admissible common endpoint there is
+$1/5$.  These examples show separately why the local degree
+conditions and the larger odd-set conditions occur.
+
+\proofpart{A quantitative test on one finite alphabet}
+The necessity proof can be made finite and stable under small
+errors in the proposed marginals.  Let
+$\operatorname{dist}_\infty(\lambda,\mathfrak M_r)$ denote the
+distance in the maximum-coordinate norm, and let total variation
+between probability laws mean the supremum of their differences
+on events.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Finite-alphabet obstruction]
+\label{flesh-profile-finite-obstruction}
+Let $r\ge2$, $L=\binom r2$, and
+$\lambda\in[0,1]^{\binom r2}$.  For an even integer $N\ge2$,
+fix a fixed-point-free involution $f$ of $[N]$, and define the
+pair probability laws
+\[
+ Q_{ij}(x,y)=\frac{1}{N^2}
+ \left[1+\lambda_{ij}
+       \left(N\mathbf1_{\{y=f(x)\}}-1\right)\right].
+\]
+Suppose a joint law on $[N]^r$ has each of its pair marginals
+within total variation $\varepsilon$ of $Q_{ij}$.  Then
+\begin{equation}\label{flesh-profile-distance-bound}
+ \operatorname{dist}_\infty(\lambda,\mathfrak M_r)
+       \le\frac{L}{N}+(L+1)\varepsilon.
+\end{equation}
+Consequently, if this distance is $\delta>0$, every even
+$N>L/\delta$ gives a single finite kernel
+$V_N(x,y)=N\mathbf1_{\{y=f(x)\}}$ for which the prescribed
+pair marginals cannot be realized exactly.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Sample $(Z_1,\ldots,Z_r)$ from the joint law and let $B$ be the
+event that two of its coordinates coincide.  Put
+$R=\sum_{i<j}(1-\lambda_{ij})$, so $0\le R\le L$.
+The diagonal has $Q_{ij}$-probability $(1-\lambda_{ij})/N$,
+because $f$ has no fixed points.  A union bound gives
+\[
+ \mathbb P(B)\le R/N+L\varepsilon.
+\]
+When $B$ does not occur, the pairs satisfying $Z_j=f(Z_i)$
+form a matching: two such pairs sharing a coordinate would
+force their other coordinates to coincide.  On $B$, choose the
+empty matching instead.  The inclusion-probability vector
+$\theta$ of this random matching belongs to $\mathfrak M_r$.
+
+For $e=\{i,j\}$, the event $A_e=\{Z_j=f(Z_i)\}$ has
+$Q_{ij}$-probability
+$\lambda_e+(1-\lambda_e)/N$.  Since
+$\theta_e=\mathbb P(A_e\setminus B)$, it follows that
+\[
+ \frac{1-\lambda_e-R}{N}-(L+1)\varepsilon
+ \le\theta_e-\lambda_e
+ \le\frac{1-\lambda_e}{N}+\varepsilon.
+\]
+Here $R\ge1-\lambda_e$.  Both absolute deviations are therefore
+at most $L/N+(L+1)\varepsilon$, proving
+\eqref{flesh-profile-distance-bound}.  The final assertion is
+the case $\varepsilon=0$.
+\end{proof}
+
+Thus a violation separated from the matching polytope by $\delta$
+has a finite witness with alphabet size of order $r^2/\delta$.
+For a fixed alphabet the same argument gives the explicit lower
+bound $(\delta-L/N)/(L+1)$ on the maximum pair-marginal error,
+whenever that expression is positive.  This tests realizability
+of the whole profile, rather than one chosen inequality.
+
