+\label{density-worked-examples}
+Let $U$ be a symmetric $[0,1]$-valued $r$-kernel of mean $p$, write
+$d(x)=\mathbb E[U\mid X_1=x]$, and let $M=M_U$ be its pair marginal.
+For the star $S_k=K_{1,k}$, where $k\ge1$, integrating each leaf gives the exact identity
+\[
+ t(S_k^{[r]},U)=\mathbb E d(X)^k\ge p^k.
+\]
+In particular,
+\[
+ t(P_3^{[r]},U)-p^2=\operatorname{Var}(d).
+\]
+Thus the first nontrivial tree example measures the degree variance
+exactly.  For the adjacency kernel of a finite host $G$ on $n$ vertices,
+$d(v)=(r-1)!\deg_G(v)/n^{r-1}$, so this is the variance of its
+normalized vertex degrees.  For $r\ge3$, the star expansion is an obligatory assembly of $k$ single-edge
+atoms at one point; it has no cyclic atom.  For a regular kernel its
+density is exactly the random benchmark, regardless of $r$.
