+\subsection{From atoms to arbitrary linear patterns}
+
+Private graph expansions have two shared vertices on each cyclic edge.
+For a general finite linear hypergraph, the corresponding parameter is
+the number of vertices of an edge that also lie in another edge.
+The projection argument extends to these larger shared sets.
+This includes assemblies in which a private vertex of one atom
+is identified with a vertex of another atom; the resulting pattern
+need not itself be a private graph expansion.
+
+The counting theorem of \citet[Theorem~4]{knrs2010} treats fixed
+uniformity under a quasirandomness assumption on the host.  The
+estimates here hold for every bounded symmetric host kernel and
+track the uniformity through the number of shared vertices.
+They use the higher terms of the same Hoeffding decomposition
+\citep{hoeffding1948}.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Higher-coordinate projection bound]
+\label{density-higher-projection}
+Let $r\ge2$, let $U:\Omega^r\to[0,1]$ be symmetric, let $p=\mathbb EU$, and
+let $U_b$ be its marginal on any $b$ coordinates, where $0\le b\le r$.
+Then
+\[
+ \|U_b-p\|_2^2\le\frac br\,p(1-p).
+\]
+If every one-coordinate marginal of $U$ equals $p$, then
+\[
+ \|U_b-p\|_2^2
+ \le\frac{b(b-1)}{r(r-1)}p(1-p).
+\]
+In particular the latter marginal is constant when $b\le1$.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+For each coordinate set $A\subseteq[r]$, recursively define
+\[
+ u_A=\mathbb E[U\mid X_A]-\sum_{B\subsetneq A}u_B.
+\]
+Here $u_\varnothing=p$.  Each nonconstant $u_A$ has zero integral
+in every coordinate of $A$, so these functions are mutually
+orthogonal.  Symmetry makes $\|u_A\|_2^2=v_j$ depend only on
+$j=|A|$.  Consequently
+\[
+ \operatorname{Var}U=\sum_{j=1}^r\binom rjv_j,
+ \qquad
+ \|U_b-p\|_2^2=\sum_{j=1}^b\binom bjv_j.
+\]
+For $1\le j\le b$ the binomial ratio is at most $b/r$; for
+$2\le j\le b$ it is at most $b(b-1)/(r(r-1))$.  Apply
+$\operatorname{Var}U\le p(1-p)$, and in the regular case use
+$v_1=0$.
+\end{proof}
+
+For a finite simple linear $r$-uniform hypergraph $H$, write
+\[
+ b_e(H)=|\{x\in e:\deg_H(x)\ge2\}|,
+ \qquad b(H)=\max_{e\in E(H)}b_e(H).
+\]
+If $H$ has $m\ge1$ edges, then
+$b(H)\le\min\{r,m-1\}$: each other edge can share at most one
+vertex with a given edge.  The Berge girth of $H$ is half the
+length of a shortest cycle in its incidence graph.  For linear
+$H$ it is at least three whenever it is finite.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Uniform approximation for linear hypergraphs]
+\label{density-linear-approximation}
+Let $H$ be a finite simple linear $r$-uniform hypergraph with
+$m\ge1$ edges, where $r\ge2$.  Put $b_e=b_e(H)$ and $b=b(H)$.
+For every symmetric $U:\Omega^r\to[0,1]$ of mean $p$,
+\begin{align}
+ |t(H,U)-p^m|
+ &\le\frac{p(1-p)}r
+       \sum_{\{e,f\}\subseteq E(H)}\sqrt{b_eb_f}
+       \label{density-linear-general}\\
+ &\le\binom m2\frac br\,p(1-p).
+\end{align}
+Suppose in addition that every one-coordinate marginal equals $p$.
+If $H$ has no Berge cycle, then $t(H,U)=p^m$.  Otherwise, writing
+$g$ for its Berge girth, one has
+\begin{equation}\label{density-linear-regular}
+ |t(H,U)-p^m|
+ \le(2^m-1)
+   \left(\frac{b(b-1)}{r(r-1)}p(1-p)\right)^{g/2}.
+\end{equation}
+Both conclusions hold for finite simple host hypergraphs with the
+manuscript's homomorphism-density normalization; regularity of the
+kernel corresponds to regularity of the host.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Integrate every vertex that occurs in only one edge of $H$.
+The factor on an edge $e$ becomes the marginal $U_{b_e}$ on its
+shared vertices.  Put $D_e=U_{b_e}-p$ and interpolate by
+\[
+ f(s)=\mathbb E\prod_{e\in E(H)}(p+sD_e),\qquad 0\le s\le1.
+\]
+Every interpolated factor lies in $[0,1]$.  The first derivative
+at zero vanishes, and the term of $f''(s)$ indexed by ordered
+distinct edges $e,f$ has absolute value at most
+$\|D_e\|_2\|D_f\|_2$, by Cauchy--Schwarz on the full product
+space.  Taylor's integral remainder and
+Lemma~\ref{density-higher-projection} prove
+\eqref{density-linear-general}.  The case $m=1$ is exact.
+
+Now assume regularity and expand the original product in $U-p$:
+\[
+ t(H,U)-p^m
+  =\sum_{\varnothing\ne A\subseteq E(H)}p^{m-|A|}
+      \mathbb E\prod_{e\in A}(U(X_e)-p).
+\]
+For each $A$, integrate vertices lying in exactly one selected edge.
+The resulting factor on $e\in A$ is
+$D_{e,A}=U_{b_e(A)}-p$, where $b_e(A)$ counts its vertices shared
+with another selected edge.  If $b_e(A)\le1$, this factor is zero.
+Otherwise the incidence graph on the selected edges and their
+shared vertices has all degrees at least two, so it contains a
+cycle.  This also proves that every nonempty term vanishes when
+$H$ has no Berge cycle.
+
+Choose a shortest Berge cycle among the selected edges, of length
+$\ell\ge g$.  Linearity and minimality imply that its edges meet
+each other exactly as a loose cycle: consecutive edges meet in
+their specified cycle vertex, and nonconsecutive edges are
+disjoint.  An extra incidence with an existing cycle vertex is a
+chord of the shortest incidence cycle.  A shared vertex outside
+the cycle would give a two-incidence shortcut between two cycle
+edges; combining it with a shortest arc gives a shorter cycle
+when the edges are nonconsecutive.  An additional intersection of
+consecutive edges contradicts linearity.  For a triangle, linearity and the
+three distinct cycle vertices also exclude a vertex in all three
+edges.
+
+Since $|D_{e,A}|\le1$, discard all factors not on this cycle after
+taking absolute values.  Each remaining variable lies in at most
+two cycle factors.  Integrating those that occur in only one
+factor leaves a cyclic product of two-variable nonnegative
+kernels $K_1,\ldots,K_\ell$.  Jensen's inequality and the
+projection bound give
+\[
+ \|K_i\|_2\le\|D_{e_i,A}\|_2
+ \le q:=\left(\frac{b(b-1)}{r(r-1)}p(1-p)\right)^{1/2}
+ \le\frac12.
+\]
+The cyclic integral is at most $\prod_i\|K_i\|_2$.  One way to
+see this is to represent each kernel by its matrix on
+$L^2(\Omega)$: the integral is the trace of their product, bounded
+by the product of their Hilbert--Schmidt norms.  Indeed,
+$|\operatorname{tr}(AB)|\le\|A\|_{\mathrm{HS}}
+\|B\|_{\mathrm{HS}}$, while
+$\|AB\|_{\mathrm{HS}}\le\|A\|_{\mathrm{HS}}
+\|B\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le\|A\|_{\mathrm{HS}}
+\|B\|_{\mathrm{HS}}$; iteration gives the assertion.  Thus the term indexed by $A$ is
+at most $q^\ell\le q^g$ in absolute value.  Sum over the at most
+$2^m-1$ nonempty subsets to obtain
+\eqref{density-linear-regular}.
+
+The adjacency kernel of a finite simple host is symmetric and
+$[0,1]$-valued.  Its one-coordinate marginal is constant exactly
+when all host vertices have the same degree.  Applying the kernel
+statements gives the asserted finite-host versions directly.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{proposition}[Sharpness for every cyclic incidence pattern]
+\label{density-linear-sharpness}
+Fix a finite linear incidence pattern with $m$ named edges and
+finitely many shared vertices, each belonging to at least two
+edges.  Complete its edges to size $r$ with distinct private
+vertices, obtaining $H_r$ for all sufficiently large $r$.
+If the fixed pattern has Berge girth $g<\infty$, then the order
+$r^{-g}$ in \eqref{density-linear-regular} is optimal for this
+pattern, already at density $p=1/2$.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Use the regular cosine kernels from the proof of
+Theorem~\ref{density-regular-girth}.  Write
+$\theta=r^{-1/2}$, $c=\cos\theta$, and
+\[
+ U_r(x_1,\ldots,x_r)
+ =\frac12+\frac14\left[
+    \cos\!\left(\theta\sum_{i=1}^r x_i\right)-c^r\right]
+\]
+on independent uniform signs.  After integrating private
+coordinates, its marginal on any $b$ coordinates is
+\[
+ (U_r)_b=\frac12+
+  \sum_{\substack{\varnothing\ne S\subseteq[b]\\|S|\ \mathrm{even}}}
+      a_{r,|S|}\prod_{i\in S}x_i,
+ \qquad
+ a_{r,j}=\frac14(-1)^{j/2}\sin^j\theta\,c^{r-j}.
+\]
+For each fixed even $j\ge2$, $a_{r,j}=O(r^{-j/2})$, and
+$a_{r,2}=-b_r$ with $b_r\sim e^{-1/2}/(4r)$.
+
+Choose a term from this expansion at each hyperedge of $H_r$.
+Averaging the independent signs makes the term zero unless every
+shared vertex has even selected degree.  Each selected hyperedge
+also has even degree, by the displayed marginal formula.  The
+surviving terms therefore correspond precisely to even-degree
+edge subsets of the fixed incidence graph.  Every nonempty such
+subset contains a cycle and uses at least $2g$ incidences.
+Subsets using exactly $2g$ incidences are precisely the shortest
+incidence cycles.  If $N_g$ is their number, they contribute
+\[
+ N_g(-b_r)^g2^{-(m-g)}.
+\]
+Every other surviving subset uses at least $2g+2$ incidences and
+contributes $O(r^{-g-1})$.  The incidence pattern is fixed, so
+there are only finitely many terms, independently of $r$.
+Consequently
+\[
+ t(H_r,U_r)-2^{-m}
+  =N_g(-b_r)^g2^{-(m-g)}+O_H(r^{-g-1}).
+\]
+Since $N_g>0$, its absolute value is of order $r^{-g}$.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{corollary}[Counting obligatory assemblies in regular hosts]
+\label{density-obligatory-regular}
+Fix $r\ge3$, and let $H\in\mathcal B_r$ have $m\ge1$ edges.
+For every symmetric regular $[0,1]$-valued $r$-kernel $U$ of mean
+$p$, a Berge-acyclic $H$ satisfies $t(H,U)=p^m$.  Otherwise, with
+$b=b(H)$,
+\[
+ |t(H,U)-p^m|
+ \le(2^m-1)
+       \left(\frac{b(b-1)p(1-p)}{r(r-1)}\right)^2.
+\]
+In particular, with $m$ fixed the error is $O_m(r^{-4})$, uniformly
+over all obligatory patterns with $m$ edges and all such kernels.
+The same assertions hold in finite simple regular hosts.
+\end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+The classification makes $H$ linear and every Berge cycle even.
+Thus a finite Berge girth is at least four.  Apply
+Theorem~\ref{density-linear-approximation}, using $q\le1/2$ from
+its proof and $b\le m-1$.
+\end{proof}
+
+This conclusion concerns the full assembled pattern.  Its exponent
+improves to the actual shortest Berge-cycle length, and
+Proposition~\ref{density-linear-sharpness} proves optimality for
+every fixed cyclic incidence pattern.  In particular, the exponent
+four cannot be increased for the obligatory class: the single atom
+$C_4^{[r]}$ already attains this absolute error scale.  These are
+two-sided error estimates; their sharpness examples do not assert a
+negative Sidorenko deficit for bipartite atoms.
+
+\subsection{Degree fluctuations and one-point assembly}
+
+The regular estimate has a useful stable form.  Degree variation
+contributes its variance, while the remaining error retains the
+Berge-girth exponent.  The explicit constant in the next theorem
+is chosen to keep this separation uniform over all finite spaces.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Degree variance and cyclic error]
+\label{density-almost-regular}
+Let $r\ge2$ and let $H$ be a finite simple linear $r$-uniform
+hypergraph with $m\ge1$ edges.  Put $b=b(H)$.  Let
+$U:\Omega^r\to[0,1]$ be symmetric, with mean $p$, and write
+\[
+ d(x)=\mathbb E[U\mid X_1=x],\qquad
+ h=d-p,\qquad \delta^2=\|h\|_2^2.
+\]
+Define
+\[
+ q^2=\frac{b(b-1)}{r(r-1)}
+          \bigl[p(1-p)-r\delta^2\bigr],\qquad
+ C_{m,b}=\bigl[(b+1)(b+2)\bigr]^m.
+\]
+The bracket is nonnegative.  If $H$ has no Berge cycle, then
+\begin{equation}\label{density-almost-regular-forest}
+ |t(H,U)-p^m|\le C_{m,b}\delta^2.
+\end{equation}
+Otherwise, if $g$ is its Berge girth, then
+\begin{equation}\label{density-almost-regular-cycle}
+ |t(H,U)-p^m|\le C_{m,b}\bigl(\delta^2+q^g\bigr).
+\end{equation}
+No optimality is claimed for $C_{m,b}$.  Since $b\le m-1$, it is
+at most $[m(m+1)]^m$.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+For a coordinate set $S$ of size at most $b$, let $U_S$ be the
+corresponding marginal and put
+\[
+ K_S=U_S-p-\sum_{x\in S}h(X_x).
+\]
+The higher-coordinate orthogonal decomposition gives
+\begin{equation}\label{density-higher-remainder}
+ \|K_S\|_2^2
+ \le\frac{|S|(|S|-1)}{r(r-1)}
+        \bigl[p(1-p)-r\delta^2\bigr]\le q^2.
+\end{equation}
+Indeed the first-coordinate projections contribute exactly
+$r\delta^2$ to $\operatorname{Var}U$, and the binomial-ratio
+bound applies to all the remaining orders, which are at least two.
+This also proves nonnegativity of the bracket.  We have
+$|h|\le1$, $|K_S|\le b+1=:B$, and $0\le q\le1/2$.
+For every $T\subseteq S$, conditional expectation gives
+\begin{equation}\label{density-remainder-projection}
+ \mathbb E[K_S\mid X_T]=K_T.
+\end{equation}
+In particular $K_T=0$ when $|T|\le1$.  This statement concerns
+the marginal on $T$; it does not assert that integrating any one
+coordinate of $K_S$ while fixing all the others gives zero.
+
+Integrate the private vertices of $H$ and let $S_e$ be the shared
+vertices of an edge $e$.  Expand each remaining factor as
+\[
+ U_{S_e}=p+\sum_{x\in S_e}h(X_x)+K_{S_e}.
+\]
+There are at most $(b+2)^m$ expansion terms.  The term selecting
+$p$ on every edge is $p^m$.  In each other term let $a$ be the
+number of selected $h$ factors and $t$ the number of selected
+$K$ factors; ignore the constant coefficient $p^{m-a-t}\le1$.
+
+If $a\ge2$, keep two selected $h$ factors and bound all the others
+by one and every $K$ factor by $B$.  If the two kept factors use
+the same vertex, their absolute integral is $\delta^2$.  If they
+use distinct vertices, independence gives an integral
+$(\mathbb E|h|)^2\le\delta^2$.  This term is therefore at most
+$B^m\delta^2$ in absolute value.
+
+Suppose next that $a=0$ or $a=1$, marking the vertex $x$ of the
+unique $h$ factor in the latter case.  Among the selected $K$
+edges integrate every vertex that occurs in just one of these
+edges, except for the marked vertex.  Formula
+\eqref{density-remainder-projection} replaces each $K_{S_e}$ by
+the corresponding smaller $K$ kernel, still bounded by $B$ with
+$L^2$ norm at most $q$.  If its retained support has size at most
+one, the entire term vanishes.
+
+In a nonzero term the resulting incidence graph thus has every
+edge-node of degree at least two.  Every point-node also has
+degree at least two, except possibly the single marked point.
+If the marked point occurs in no selected $K$ edge, its factor
+integrates to zero.  Otherwise this incidence graph cannot be a
+forest: a finite tree with at least one edge has at least two
+leaves, whereas at most one node here can have degree one.
+This proves that all $a\le1$ terms vanish when $H$ is Berge-acyclic.
+It also shows that any remaining term contains a Berge cycle
+among the selected $K$ edges.
+
+Choose a shortest such cycle, of length $\ell\ge g$.
+As in the proof of Theorem~\ref{density-linear-approximation},
+its edge factors share variables only consecutively around the
+cycle.  If $a=0$, discard all other factors at cost $B^{t-\ell}$.
+The cyclic integral is at most the product of the $L^2$ norms,
+giving
+\[
+ |\text{term}|\le B^{t-\ell}q^\ell\le B^m q^g.
+\]
+If $a=1$, Cauchy--Schwarz first gives
+\[
+ |\text{term}|
+ \le B^{t-\ell}\delta
+      \left(\mathbb E\prod_{e\ \mathrm{on\ the\ cycle}}K_e^2\right)^{1/2}.
+\]
+The same cyclic integral bound applies to the squared kernels.
+For each factor,
+$\|K_e^2\|_2\le B\|K_e\|_2\le Bq$, so
+\[
+ |\text{term}|
+ \le B^{t-\ell/2}\delta q^{\ell/2}
+ \le\tfrac12B^m(\delta^2+q^g).
+\]
+Here we used $q\le1$ and the inequality $2uv\le u^2+v^2$.
+Summing the at most $(b+2)^m$ terms proves both assertions.
+\end{proof}
+
+For a fixed finite linear incidence pattern, $b$ and $m$ remain
+fixed as private vertices are added.  The last theorem therefore
+gives the uniform estimate
+\[
+ |t(H_r,U_r)-p_r^m|
+    =O_H\bigl(\operatorname{Var}(d_r)+r^{-g}\bigr)
+\]
+when the pattern is cyclic, and
+$O_H(\operatorname{Var}(d_r))$ when it is Berge-acyclic.
+Thus the regular $r^{-g}$ scale persists whenever the degree
+variance is $O(r^{-g})$, while the general $r^{-1}$ scale permits
+degree variance of order $r^{-1}$.  The theorem requires only
+control of the $L^2$ degree fluctuation, not pointwise regularity.
+
+For a finite host $G$ of order $n$, the same theorem applies with
+\[
+ d(v)=\frac{(r-1)!\deg_G(v)}{n^{r-1}},\qquad
+ \delta^2=\frac1n\sum_{v\in V(G)}(d(v)-p_r(G))^2.
+\]
+Thus its hypothesis and error can be read directly from the
+normalized degree sequence.
+
+The one-point operation also has an exact density formula.  For a
+rooted pattern $(A,a)$, let $a_U(x)$ be its homomorphism density
+with the image of $a$ fixed at $x$.  If $A\vee B$ identifies the
+roots of two otherwise disjoint patterns, conditional independence
+gives
+\begin{equation}\label{density-rooted-gluing}
+ t(A\vee B,U)=\mathbb E[a_U(X)b_U(X)]
+   =t(A,U)t(B,U)+\operatorname{Cov}(a_U(X),b_U(X)).
+\end{equation}
+For two copies of the same rooted pattern the covariance is a
+variance.  A single-edge atom has rooted density $d$, so regularity
+removes this extra term when edge atoms are attached.  The next
+example shows what can happen for cyclic atoms, even when the atom
+list and attachment excess profile are fixed.
+
+\begin{remark}[The attachment point can change the density]
+\label{flesh-assembly-root-example}
+Canonical atoms and their attachment multiplicities do not determine
+homomorphism density, even in a regular kernel.  Fix $r\ge3$ and
+let $B=C_4^{[r]}$.  Form $H_{\rm c}$ by identifying a core vertex
+of one copy of $B$ with a core vertex of another copy, and form
+$H_{\rm p}$ by instead identifying two private vertices.
+Both systems have the same two canonical atoms, one joint of atom
+multiplicity two, and hence the same attachment excess profile
+$(1)$.  Both have $8$ edges, $8r-9$ vertices and cyclic rank $2$.
+Nevertheless, a single bounded regular kernel distinguishes their
+densities.
+
+Take the uniform probability space $\Omega=\{-1,0,1\}$, write
+$s=\mathbb E Z^2=2/3$, and choose $0<p<1$ and
+\[
+ 0<\varepsilon\le
+       \frac{\min\{p,1-p\}}{\binom r2}.
+\]
+The symmetric kernel
+\[
+ U_r(z_1,\ldots,z_r)
+      =p+\varepsilon\sum_{i<j}z_i z_j
+\]
+takes values in $[0,1]$, has mean $p$, and has every
+one-coordinate marginal equal to $p$, since $\mathbb E Z=0$.
+Its two- and three-coordinate marginals are
+\[
+ M(x,y)=p+\varepsilon xy,
+ \qquad N(x,y,z)=p+\varepsilon(xy+xz+yz).
+\]
+
+Let $a_{\rm c}(z)$ and $a_{\rm p}(z)$ denote the density of $B$
+when a specified core or private vertex, respectively, is fixed
+at $z$, while all other vertices are sampled independently.
+Integrating the private vertices and expanding the four-cycle gives
+\[
+ a_{\rm c}(z)=p^4+\varepsilon^4s^3z^2,
+ \qquad t(B,U_r)=p^4+\varepsilon^4s^4.
+\]
+Indeed, every proper nonempty edge subset of the cycle has an
+unfixed degree-one vertex, whose coordinate has mean zero.
+
+For a fixed private vertex $z$ on the edge with core endpoints
+$x,y$, let $K_3(x,y)$ be the integral of the three $M$-factors
+on the complementary path.  Its row and column integrals are
+$p^3$, because $M$ has constant row integral $p$.
+As $N(x,y,z)=M(x,y)+\varepsilon z(x+y)$, it follows that
+\[
+ \begin{split}
+ a_{\rm p}(z)
+ &=\mathbb E_{x,y}N(x,y,z)K_3(x,y)\\
+ &=t(B,U_r)+\varepsilon z p^3
+                      (\mathbb E x+\mathbb E y)
+ =t(B,U_r).
+ \end{split}
+\]
+This calculation also applies when $r=3$, when the selected edge
+has no other private vertices to integrate.
+
+Conditioning on the common vertex in a one-point amalgamation
+makes the remaining variables in its two atoms independent.
+Consequently
+\[
+ \begin{split}
+ t(H_{\rm c},U_r)-t(H_{\rm p},U_r)
+ &=\mathbb E a_{\rm c}(Z)^2-
+                          \bigl(t(B,U_r)\bigr)^2\\
+ &=\operatorname{Var}(a_{\rm c}(Z))
+ =\varepsilon^8s^6\operatorname{Var}(Z^2)
+ =\frac{128}{6561}\varepsilon^8>0.
+ \end{split}
+\]
+Thus the location of a joint inside an atom carries density
+information that its multiplicity and the atom list do not record.
+\end{remark}
+
 \subsection{Transfer from proper-subgraph limits}
 
 The preceding estimates do not give the exact Sidorenko inequality.
 For a fixed uniformity the next lemma transfers a strict deficit,
 provided the witness family has sufficiently controlled proper
 edge-subgraph densities.
+
+There is also a finite error budget behind the limit argument.
+Let $J$ be a finite simple graph with $m\ge1$ edges, write
+$J_A=(V(J),A)$, and let $V$ be a finite symmetric nonnegative
+kernel with constant row integral one.  Suppose $0<\lambda\le1$,
+$t(J,V)\le1-\delta$ with $\delta>0$,
+and $|t(J_A,V)-1|\le\varepsilon$ for every proper edge subset.
+The expansion used in the next proof gives
+\begin{equation}\label{density-transfer-error-budget}
+ t(J,1+\lambda(V-1))
+ \le1-\delta\lambda^m+\varepsilon(1-\lambda^m).
+\end{equation}
+Indeed, the coefficients of the proper-subgraph terms are
+nonnegative and sum to $1-\lambda^m$.  Thus
+$\varepsilon(1-\lambda^m)<\delta\lambda^m$ suffices for a strict
+deficit.  At the universal coefficient $\lambda_r$, the retained
+signal has order $r^{-m}$ for a fixed full-graph deficit.  This
+explains why the proper-subgraph approximation must be chosen after
+the uniformity, and why the limit statement is not a uniform bound
+on the density or size of a finite witnessing host.
 
